@page "/media"

<CatHead Title="Media and prose">
    Content the app did not author: an attachment, an article body, or a value the reader is not
    allowed to see.
</CatHead>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Media.MediaFrame" Title="Media frame" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        A bounded box for an image or embed whose size you do not control: it has its height before
        the image loads, and it contains rather than crops, because cropping a screenshot removes
        the part somebody needed. <code>.media-frame--cover</code> is for a decorative banner, where
        cropping is the right answer; use <code>&lt;figure class="media-figure"&gt;</code> when
        there is a caption, and give the image a real <code>alt</code> either way.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Media.Prose" Title="Prose" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        Long-form text rendered from a source the app does not control — a release note, a
        knowledge-base article, an e-mail body. <a href="/markdown"><code>.markdown-body</code></a>
        covers rendered Markdown and looks identical; <code>.prose</code> is the same job when the
        HTML did not come from Markdown. <strong>The 72-character measure sits on the element
        carrying the class</strong>, so where the prose <em>is</em> a panel &mdash;
        <code>class="card-body prose"</code> &mdash; set <code>--prose-measure: none</code> on the
        box and put the class on an inner wrapper.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Media.ForeignProse" Title="HTML from somewhere else" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.prose--foreign</code> adds <strong>containment</strong> on top of
        <code>.prose</code>'s typography: what a 3000px-wide table, an unbroken 400-character URL or
        a 4000px image does to the card it landed in. Add it to anything sanitized rather than
        authored — a ticketing system's journal entry, a forwarded mail body, a CMS field — and note
        that it contains but does not sanitize: the same server-side pass has to add
        <code>tabindex="0"</code> to a table that scrolls, or a keyboard cannot reach the off-screen
        columns, and <code>role="table"</code> to put back the role
        <code>display: block</code> drops.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Media.RedactedValues" Title="Redacted values" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        A value deliberately withheld — a secret, a masked identifier, a field the reader's role
        cannot see. <strong>It must not merely look hidden:</strong> this class marks a placeholder
        the server sent <em>in place of</em> the value, so if the real text is in the HTML it is not
        redacted, whatever it looks like.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Media.Gallery" Title="A gallery of attachments" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.gallery</code> is a breakpoint-free grid of thumbnails. Each tile is a real
        <code>&lt;button&gt;</code> or <code>&lt;a&gt;</code> directly inside it — never a
        <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> with a click handler — and every frame is square whatever aspect
        modifier it carries, so a row of mixed attachments lines up.
    </p>
</CatExample>
